1. Why do kiosk threads finish unpredictably?
-> Even though the kiosk threads are started in a fixed sequence, the thread scheduler decides when each thread gets CPU
    time. Since each thread also sleeps for a random amount of time, they can wake up and finish in different orders each
    time.

2. What are the six Java thread states?
-> The six states are NEW, RUNNABLE, BLOCKED, WAITING, TIMED_WAITING, and TERMINATED. A thread moves from RUNNABLE to
    TIMED_WAITING when it performs a timed operation such as Thread.sleep(1000), causing it to pause for a specified amount
    of time.

3. Why doesn't calling run() create concurrency?
-> Calling run() directly simply executes the method on the current thread, just like a normal method call. start() is
    what creates a new thread and then causes that new thread to execute run(), allowing concurrent execution.

C3
1) Explain the difference between wait()/notify() (used with synchronized blocks) and sleep() — specifically, what happens
    to the lock in each case.
->  wait() releases the object's intrinsic lock and puts the thread into a waiting state until it is notified,
    while sleep() pauses the thread for a period of time without releasing any lock it currently holds.

2)  Why is notifyAll() generally safer than notify() when more than one type of thread might be waiting on the same object?
->  notifyAll() is generally safer than notify() when different types of threads may be waiting because it wakes all waiting
    threads, allowing each to re-check its condition; notify() might wake a thread whose condition is still false, leaving
    the correct waiting thread blocked.